.\" Man page for sshcheck
.\" Contact: rom@romab.com
.TH SSHCHECK 1 "February 2026" "sshcheck 2.0.0" "Security Audit Tools"
.SH NAME
sshcheck \- SSH Security Audit Client for testing login capabilities
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sshcheck
[\fB\-t\fR \fIHOST\fR | \fB\-T\fR \fIFILE\fR]
[\fB\-u\fR \fIUSER\fR | \fB\-U\fR \fIFILE\fR]
[\fB\-p\fR \fIPASS\fR | \fB\-P\fR \fIFILE\fR]
[\fB\-k\fR \fIKEYFILE\fR | \fB\-K\fR \fIFILE\fR]
[\fB\-C\fR \fICOMBOFILE\fR]
[\fB\-\-port\fR \fIPORT\fR | \fB\-\-port-file\fR \fIFILE\fR]
[\fB\-\-proxy\fR \fIURL\fR]
[\fB\-\-no-color\fR]
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sshcheck
is a security audit tool designed for testing SSH login capabilities across multiple hosts.
It attempts to authenticate to target SSH servers using provided credentials (passwords or
SSH keys) and captures initial server output including welcome messages, banners, and shell prompts.
.PP
The tool also performs OS/software fingerprinting, enumerates SSH algorithms to detect weaknesses,
collects host key fingerprints, and checks detected software versions against known CVE databases.
.PP
This tool is intended for authorized penetration testing, security assessments, and
network audits. Always ensure you have proper authorization before scanning any systems.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS "Target Specification"
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-target " " \fIHOST\fR
Target host(s) to scan. Can be specified multiple times. Accepts:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Single IP address (e.g., 192.168.1.1)
.IP \(bu 2
CIDR range (e.g., 192.168.1.0/24)
.IP \(bu 2
IP range (e.g., 192.168.1.1-254)
.IP \(bu 2
Hostname (e.g., server.example.com)
.RE
.TP
.BR \-T ", " \-\-target-file " " \fIFILE\fR
File containing target hosts, one per line. Supports the same formats as \fB-t\fR.
Lines starting with # are treated as comments.
.SS "Username Specification"
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-user " " \fIUSER\fR
Username(s) to try. Can be specified multiple times for multiple usernames.
.TP
.BR \-U ", " \-\-user-file " " \fIFILE\fR
File containing usernames, one per line. Lines starting with # are treated as comments.
.SS "Password Specification"
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-password " " \fIPASS\fR
Password(s) to try. Can be specified multiple times for multiple passwords.
When used with \fB-k\fR, the password is used as the passphrase for encrypted keys.
.TP
.BR \-P ", " \-\-password-file " " \fIFILE\fR
File containing passwords, one per line. Lines starting with # are treated as comments.
.SS "Key Authentication"
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-key " " \fIFILE\fR
SSH private key file(s) for key-based authentication. Supports RSA, Ed25519, ECDSA,
and DSA key types. Can be specified multiple times. Use \fB-p\fR to provide a passphrase
for encrypted keys.
.TP
.BR \-K ", " \-\-key-file " " \fIFILE\fR
File containing paths to SSH private key files, one per line.
Lines starting with # are treated as comments.
.SS "Combo File"
.TP
.BR \-C ", " \-\-combo-file " " \fIFILE\fR
File containing username:password combinations, one per line. The format is
\fIusername\fR:\fIpassword\fR. Colons within passwords are supported (splits on
first colon only). Lines starting with # are treated as comments.
.SS "Port Specification"
.TP
.BR \-\-port " " \fIPORT\fR
SSH port(s) to try. Default is 22. Can be specified multiple times.
.TP
.BR \-\-port-file " " \fIFILE\fR
File containing ports, one per line. Lines starting with # are treated as comments.
.SS "Output Options"
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " " \fIFILE\fR
Save results to the specified file. The output format is determined by the \fB-f\fR option.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-format " " \fIFORMAT\fR
Output format for the results file. Valid options:
.RS
.IP \fBtext\fR
Human-readable text format (default)
.IP \fBjson\fR
JSON format with full scan details, vulnerabilities, and algorithm analysis
.IP \fBcsv\fR
Comma-separated values for spreadsheet import
.RE
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Enable verbose output with detailed error messages and debug information.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress progress output, only show final summary.
.TP
.BR \-\-no-color
Disable colored terminal output. Useful when piping output to files or
when using terminals that do not support ANSI color codes.
.SS "Performance Options"
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-threads " " \fINUM\fR
Number of concurrent threads. Default is 1 (sequential scanning).
Higher values increase scan speed but may trigger rate limiting.
Recommended range: 1-50.
.TP
.BR \-\-timeout " " \fISECONDS\fR
Connection timeout in seconds. Default is 10 seconds.
Increase for slow networks or decrease for faster scanning.
.SS "Proxy Options"
.TP
.BR \-\-proxy " " \fIURL\fR
Route all SSH connections through a proxy. Supported proxy types:
.RS
.IP \fBsocks5://host:port\fR
SOCKS5 proxy (requires PySocks library)
.IP \fBsocks4://host:port\fR
SOCKS4 proxy (requires PySocks library)
.IP \fBhttp://host:port\fR
HTTP CONNECT proxy
.RE
.PP
Default ports: 1080 for SOCKS, 8080 for HTTP. SOCKS proxy support requires the
PySocks library (\fBpip install pysocks\fR).
.SS "Other Options"
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Display version information and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display help message and exit.
.SH INPUT FILE FORMATS
.SS "Target File Format"
The target file should contain one target per line. Supported formats:
.PP
.nf
.RS
# Comment line (ignored)
192.168.1.1
192.168.1.0/24
10.0.0.1-254
server.example.com
.RE
.fi
.SS "Username File Format"
The username file should contain one username per line:
.PP
.nf
.RS
# Common usernames
root
admin
administrator
user
.RE
.fi
.SS "Password File Format"
The password file should contain one password per line:
.PP
.nf
.RS
# Common passwords
password
admin
123456
password123
.RE
.fi
.SS "Combo File Format"
The combo file should contain one username:password pair per line.
Passwords containing colons are supported (split on first colon only):
.PP
.nf
.RS
# username:password
root:password
admin:admin123
user:p@ss:w0rd
.RE
.fi
.SS "Port File Format"
The port file should contain one port number per line:
.PP
.nf
.RS
# Common SSH ports
22
2222
22222
.RE
.fi
.SH OUTPUT FILE FORMATS
.SS "Text Format"
Human-readable format containing scan statistics, successful logins,
host key fingerprints, software fingerprints, vulnerability findings,
and weak algorithm reports.
.SS "JSON Format"
Structured JSON containing:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBscan_info\fR: Program version, timestamps, duration, proxy info
.IP \(bu 2
\fBstatistics\fR: Attempt counts, success/failure rates
.IP \(bu 2
\fBresults\fR: Array of all scan results with full details including
auth_method, key_file, fingerprint_info, host_key_info, algorithm_info,
and vulnerabilities
.SS "CSV Format"
Comma-separated values with extended headers including:
.PP
host, port, username, password, auth_method, key_file, success, timestamp, banner,
initial_output, error_message, connection_time, software, software_version, os_guess,
host_key_type, host_key_bits, host_key_sha256, vulnerabilities
.SH SECURITY AUDIT FEATURES
.SS "OS/Software Fingerprinting"
sshcheck identifies SSH server software and operating system from the SSH banner string.
Recognized servers include OpenSSH (with OS detection for Ubuntu, Debian, RHEL, Fedora,
FreeBSD, Windows), Dropbear, libssh, Cisco IOS, MikroTik RouterOS, Huawei VRP, and more.
.SS "Vulnerability Matching"
Detected SSH software versions are checked against a database of known CVEs including
CVE-2024-6387 (regreSSHion), CVE-2023-48795 (Terrapin Attack), CVE-2023-38408,
CVE-2021-41617, CVE-2018-15473, and others across OpenSSH, Dropbear, and libssh.
.SS "Algorithm Weakness Detection"
sshcheck reports weak or deprecated SSH algorithms including:
.IP \(bu 2
Weak key exchange algorithms (DH group1, SHA-1 based KEX)
.IP \(bu 2
Deprecated ciphers (RC4, 3DES, CBC mode)
.IP \(bu 2
Weak MACs (MD5, SHA-1 based)
.IP \(bu 2
Deprecated host key types (DSA, SHA-1 RSA)
.SS "Host Key Fingerprints"
For each target host, sshcheck collects the server's SSH host key and reports:
.IP \(bu 2
Key type and bit length
.IP \(bu 2
SHA-256 fingerprint
.IP \(bu 2
MD5 fingerprint (colon-separated)
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
At least one successful login was found.
.TP
.B 1
No successful logins found or error occurred.
.TP
.B 130
Scan was interrupted by user (Ctrl+C).
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Scan single host with single credentials:
.B sshcheck -t 192.168.1.1 -u admin -p password123
.TP
Scan entire subnet:
.B sshcheck -t 192.168.1.0/24 -u root -p admin123
.TP
Scan using credential lists and save JSON output:
.B sshcheck -T targets.txt -U users.txt -P passwords.txt -o results.json -f json
.TP
Scan using SSH key authentication:
.B sshcheck -t 192.168.1.1 -u admin -k ~/.ssh/id_rsa
.TP
Scan with encrypted key and passphrase:
.B sshcheck -t 192.168.1.1 -u admin -k ~/.ssh/id_rsa -p my_passphrase
.TP
Scan using combo file:
.B sshcheck -t 192.168.1.1 -C combos.txt
.TP
Scan through SOCKS5 proxy (e.g., Tor):
.B sshcheck -t 192.168.1.1 -u root -p pass --proxy socks5://127.0.0.1:9050
.TP
Scan through HTTP proxy:
.B sshcheck -t 192.168.1.1 -u root -p pass --proxy http://proxy.corp.com:8080
.TP
Scan with color output disabled (for logging):
.B sshcheck -t 192.168.1.1 -u root -p pass --no-color > results.log
.TP
Scan IP range with 10 threads and 5 second timeout:
.B sshcheck -t 10.0.0.1-254 -u admin -p password -n 10 --timeout 5
.TP
Scan non-standard SSH port:
.B sshcheck -t server.example.com -u root -p secret --port 2222
.TP
Verbose scanning with multiple users and passwords:
.B sshcheck -t 192.168.1.1 -u root -u admin -p pass1 -p pass2 -v
.SH ERROR MESSAGES
.TP
.B "Authentication failed"
The username/password combination was rejected by the server.
.TP
.B "Connection timed out"
The host did not respond within the timeout period.
Check if the host is reachable and SSH service is running.
.TP
.B "Connection refused"
The target host actively refused the connection.
SSH service may not be running on the specified port.
.TP
.B "No route to host"
Network path to the target is unavailable.
Check network configuration and routing.
.TP
.B "Network is unreachable"
Cannot reach the target network.
Verify network connectivity.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I targets.txt
Example target hosts file
.TP
.I users.txt
Example usernames file
.TP
.I passwords.txt
Example passwords file
.TP
.I combos.txt
Example credential combo file
.TP
.I results.json
Default JSON output file
.SH SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
.PP
This tool is designed for authorized security testing only.
Unauthorized use against systems you do not own or have explicit
permission to test may violate computer crime laws.
.PP
Always ensure you have written authorization before performing
security assessments.
.PP
Passwords provided on the command line may be visible in process
listings. Use password files (\fB-P\fR) for sensitive credentials.
.PP
When using proxy connections, ensure you have authorization to
route traffic through the proxy server.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
.IP \(bu 2
Python 3.6 or later
.IP \(bu 2
paramiko library (pip install paramiko)
.IP \(bu 2
PySocks library (optional, for SOCKS proxy support: pip install pysocks)
